  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThe strong upper ridge centered over Arizona will remain in place\nthrough Friday with 500 mb heights hovering around 594 dm over \nsouthern California. High temperatures generally overperformed \nyesterday with daily record high maximums set in a few inland \nvalley and desert locations. This overperforming trend will have \nto compete with increased cloud cover today, courtesy of the \nremnants of Tropical Storm Iselle, so forecast highs are few \ndegrees lower than yesterday's temps. Regardless of exact \ntemperatures, hot days in combination with near-record warm nights\nwill produce major to locally extreme HeatRisk through Friday for\ninland valleys and lower deserts. Moderate to locally major \nHeatRisk is expected for the mountains, High Desert, and areas \nnear the coast.\n\nThere is a slight chance (10-20%) for thunderstorms over the\nmountains this afternoon and Friday afternoon. There is ample\nmoisture present (PWATs around 1.4-1.5\"), but the 12z KNKX sounding\nindicates the presence of substantial mid-level capping, which \nwill keep convection quite limited.\n\nBy Saturday, the ridge begins to weaken and slide eastward as a\nrobust upper trough pushes into the PNW. This will usher in a\nwelcome cooling trend across the region. Temperatures are\nexpected to return to near average by Sunday with highs further \ncooling to around 3 to 7 degrees below average early next week.\nThis pattern change will also produce breezy westerly winds for\nthe mountains and deserts and deepen the marine layer for Sunday\nand Monday.\n\nFrom next Tuesday onward, the forecast becomes more uncertain.\nThe synoptic set up will remain generally unchanged on the\nsynoptic scale with troughing over the PNW and ridging over the\nsouth-central CONUS resulting in southwesterly flow the region. \nSimultaneously, global models indicate that a tropical system \nwill pass well to the southwest early next week. The exact\nevolution of this large scale set up and the track of the tropical\nsystem will determine how much moisture will be drawn into this\nregion for the middle of next week. Most ensembles indicate a\nnotable increase in PWATs over southern California next week, but\nonly about 20-30% have any precipitation at this time.",
